,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This chapter bridges the gap between theoretical index replication and practical risk management by demonstrating how liquid equity futures can be used to synthesize, span, and forecast benchmark portfolios. We begin by constructing a synthetic S&P 500 investment and its mirror—a synthetic cash position—revealing how tracking error is dynamically bound by arbitrage bands tied to market volatility (VIX). Building on these core mechanics, we expand our scope to multi-index risk spanning, utilizing four major equity contracts (S&P 500, Nasdaq 100, Russell 2000, and Dow Jones) to map the risk-return profiles of five key style and size benchmarks, complete with out-of-sample predictions. Finally, a capstone exercise synthesizes these techniques to span the S&P Financials and Information Technology sectors, rigorously calibrating the out-of-sample forecasts to solidify the framework’s practical application.","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"N3gsnYTb4x"}],"key":"edHNFjHNri"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"🎛️ From Cost of Carry to Risk Control and Synthetic Positions","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"NKqUdLviXS"}],"key":"v32lnEQNxa"}],"identifier":"id-from-cost-of-carry-to-risk-control-and-synthetic-positions","label":"🎛️ From Cost of Carry to Risk Control and Synthetic Positions","html_id":"id-from-cost-of-carry-to-risk-control-and-synthetic-positions","implicit":true,"key":"fYEzzmhhfP"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This notebook bridges theory and application by taking the cost of carry equation developed in Chapter Two and modifying it to include a c